  "text_md": "![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n# 718 Michael R. Douglas and Cristofero Fraser-Taliente\n\n![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n500\n\n250\n\n1\n\nEfficientcube\n\n28 traj\n\n21.26\n\n20.64\n\nOptimal\n\n21.35\n\nDeepCubeA\n\n10B traj\n\nOurs@218\n\n1OOM traj (21.15)\n\nOurs@219\n\n1OOM traj (21.06)\n\n103\n\n105\n\n106\n\n108\n\n250\n\n104\n\n10'\n\nFrequency\n\nNumber of nodes\n\n1000 initial states randomly scrambled between 1000-10000 times. We report results with a ball radius R = 5 , and no T -calibration. For beam width 2 7 or greater, we are able to solve all states.\n\n![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n1\n\n500\n\n250\n\n1.92\n\n1\n\n10 '\n\n10 '\n\n500\n\nFrequency\n\nVumher of nodes expanded\n\nFigure 2: Average solution length vs beam width. We find a best fit given by E = 41 . 9 log 2 ( B ) − 1 − 1 . 92 , where B is the beam width (trivially related to the number of nodes). Excesses are always even due to parity. We report results with a ball radius R = 5 , and no T -calibration.\n\n![](<2503.05558_pg18_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n",
